# Tracked example of the untracked .env.production (OQ12b: .env* stays
# gitignored; this file documents the full prod-tier surface). Fresh machine:
#   cp .env.production.example .env.production
# Real secrets (Google OAuth, NextAuth, Gemini, …) live in the untracked .env.
DATABASE_URL="file:./prod.db"
PULSAR_URL="http://localhost:3103"
NEXTAUTH_URL="https://mc.salsquared.xyz"

# SQLite-backed L2 cache so withCache entries survive PM2 restarts. Dev has
# this set in .env.development; prod was missing it, which meant every restart
# blew away the satellite/launches/etc. caches and hammered Celestrak — they'd
# then 403 us with "GP data has not updated since your last successful
# download" because dev (same outbound IP) had fetched within the window.
CACHE_BACKEND="sqlite"
# Real Gmail emission ON in production — drives application-side notifications
# (OFFER / REJECTION / INTERVIEW_SCHEDULED / ASSESSMENT_REQUESTED) into your inbox.
EMAIL_ENABLED=1
# Master app→Google-Calendar write switch, mirroring EMAIL_ENABLED.
GCAL_SYNC_ENABLED=1

# arXiv pacing — this is now the SINGLE SHARED rate across ALL processes (dev +
# prod + both schedulers), enforced by the Layer 3 cross-tier token bucket in
# data/arxiv-bucket.db (one paced line, not per-process). ~14/min stays under
# arXiv's "1 per 3s" soft policy (~20/min). If the shared file can't open, each
# process falls back to a per-process bucket at HALF this rate. NOTE: this value
# changed meaning from per-process (was 7) to shared — see
# docs/arxiv-rate-limit-fix.html Layer 3. Restart with --update-env to apply.
ARXIV_RATE_PER_MIN=14
# arXiv recovery cooldown (Layer 4) — after an observed 429 / "Rate exceeded",
# ALL arXiv calls stop for this long (cross-tier) so the IP block can clear,
# then one probe re-arms or recovers. 15 min default; far longer than any arXiv
# throttle window. See docs/arxiv-rate-limit-fix.html Layer 4.
ARXIV_COOLDOWN_MS=900000

# Gemini paid-tier (2026-05-20). Default in code is 12/min (free-tier safe);
# we're on paid tier so backfill / first-time scans aren't bottlenecked on the
# bucket. Sequential ingest can't usefully go above ~120 RPM anyway — Gemini's
# own response latency (~1-2 s/call) dominates past that. 60 is a 5× raise
# at negligible cost (~$0.0002/email × Flash-Lite pricing).
GEMINI_RATE_PER_MIN=60

PUBSUB_AUDIENCE=https://mc.salsquared.xyz/api/gmail/webhook

# Cloudflare Access JWT verification — Layer 2 of the header-authenticity fix
# (lib/access-jwt.ts; docs/multi-user-crew.html §2.7, P4.2). The team domain
# gives the JWKS at https://<domain>/cdn-cgi/access/certs; CF_ACCESS_AUD is THIS
# hostname's Access application id (mc.salsquared.xyz) — an identifier, not a
# secret, and DIFFERENT from dev's.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED, unlike every cache and rate bucket in this repo: unset or stale
# values do not degrade, they deny EVERY request on this tier (401), the owner
# included, with the loopback break-glass escapes as the only way back in. An
# AUD rotates when its Access app is RECREATED (not when it is edited), so
# re-reading it is part of any app rebuild:
#   curl -sI https://mc.salsquared.xyz/   # → Location: …/login/…?kid=<AUD>
# Both values are asserted by scripts/tests/hermetic/access-jwt-smoke.ts, so a
# rotation surfaces in the pre-push gate rather than in production.
CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=salsquared.cloudflareaccess.com
CF_ACCESS_AUD=2ed7bc0e82620032d1b9b2cd3ef1f028c554cf3635e147a73f16f158cfb4b10f

# Per-crew daily AI credit (docs/multi-user-crew.html §2.9 / OQ7a, P2.5;
# lib/ai/quota.ts). The seven LLM-bearing routes — resumes, resumes/specialize,
# profile/bullets/assist, profile/tagline/draft, profile/import,
# discovery/suggest, watchlists/[id]/run — spend one credit per invocation and
# return 429 { used, limit } once a crew member is out. The OWNER IS EXEMPT and
# is never metered at all. Default in code is 20 — set here only to override; 0
# is honoured and means "crew get no interactive AI".
#
# What it bounds: a crew member looping "regenerate" until the owner's Gmail
# ingest classifier starves behind them on the shared Gemini bucket (this tier
# sets GEMINI_RATE_PER_MIN=60 above; the code default is 12). It
# counts ROUTE INVOCATIONS, not Gemini calls (one POST /api/resumes can make
# three), and it is a route-boundary cap, so the scheduler's
# classifyEmploymentTypes sits deliberately OUTSIDE it — bounded instead by the
# PAIR below: CREW_WATCHLIST_LIMIT caps how MANY standing crawls a crew member
# holds, CREW_MIN_SCHEDULE_MINUTES caps how OFTEN each one runs. Count alone is
# not a bound (a 1-minute cadence turns 5 watchlists into ~7,200 crawls/day), so
# both must stand for the exemption to hold. Recorded v1 decisions, not gaps.
# Read per request — apply with `pm2 restart … --update-env`.
# CREW_AI_DAILY_LIMIT=20

# Per-crew watchlist cap (docs/multi-user-crew.html OQ10a, P2.6.1). POST
# /api/watchlists rejects a crew member's create with 400 once they hold this
# many watchlists; the OWNER is exempt. Default in code is 5 — set here only to
# override. This is not cosmetic: a watchlist is a standing claim on the
# scheduler (defaultLoadDue has no ceiling), on this box's single outbound IP,
# and on the owner's Gemini key, because the scheduler's classifyEmploymentTypes
# sits deliberately outside the per-user AI credit cap. Raising it raises all
# three. Read at module load — apply with `pm2 restart … --update-env`.
# CREW_WATCHLIST_LIMIT=5

# Per-crew FLOOR under Watchlist.scheduleMinutes — the minimum gap between two
# automatic crawls of one crew-owned watchlist (lib/watchlists/schedule-floor.ts).
# POST and PATCH /api/watchlists both reject a crew member's sub-floor cadence
# with 400; the OWNER is exempt, and legitimately runs minute-scale cadences to
# debug a fetcher. Default in code is 60 — set here only to override.
# Why it exists: the schema only asked for a positive int, so crew could set
# scheduleMinutes=1 on each of their CREW_WATCHLIST_LIMIT watchlists — ~7,200
# crawls/day instead of ~30 — spending the owner's Gemini key via the
# scheduler's classifyEmploymentTypes, crowding the shared Gemini bucket the
# owner's Gmail ingest queues in, and hammering Greenhouse/Lever/LinkedIn from
# this box's single residential IP. 60 equals the fastest cadence the UI itself
# offers, so the floor is invisible to every real crew flow; lowering it
# re-opens that ceiling. Read per request — apply with `pm2 restart … --update-env`.
# CREW_MIN_SCHEDULE_MINUTES=60

# Lives in the untracked .env (shared across tiers), not here — gates Google
# sign-in to the listed addresses. FAILS CLOSED: unset or blank denies EVERY
# sign-in, the owner included, and returns NextAuth's `Configuration` error
# (not `AccessDenied`) so "fix your env" cannot be mistaken for "you are not on
# the list"; instrumentation.ts also logs it at boot. It fails closed because a
# User row IS the authorization to use this app since the owner/crew work — a
# blank value would let anyone who clears the Cloudflare Access edge
# self-provision a crew row and bypass the deliberate two-step provisioning.
# ALLOWED_SIGNIN_EMAILS=<owner@example.com>

# BREAK-GLASS — loopback-only owner fallback (lib/viewer.ts:tryBreakGlass;
# docs/multi-user-crew.html §2.3, P1.2.3). OFF by default, and this is the tier
# where that matters. Arm it only while recovering, then unset it.
#
# WHAT ARMING IT DOES: a loopback request carrying NO Cloudflare Access identity
# header resolves to the OWNER — skipping both the Access-JWT verification and
# the User-row lookup. It is an identity bypass by design. Loopback is detected
# from X-Forwarded-For (no socket is available to the guards), so anything that
# can open a TCP connection to :3101 and forge that header becomes the owner;
# what bounds it is that prod binds 127.0.0.1 and this stays unset. One warn per
# process lands in the in-app log viewer while it is armed.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: the tunnel or Cloudflare Access is down (or CF_ACCESS_AUD went
# stale after an Access app rebuild — a fail-closed total lockout) and the owner
# needs the app from this box's console. Unlike the dev var it is NOT gated on
# NODE_ENV, so it fires on any tier. Apply with
# `pm2 restart mission-control --update-env`; unset and restart once the edge is
# healthy.
# MC_PROD_LOOPBACK_OWNER=1
#
# Its dev-tier sibling MC_DEV_LOOPBACK_OWNER (integration-suite escape) is a
# no-op here — hard-gated on NODE_ENV !== 'production'. See
# .env.development.example.

# OWNER_EMAIL — RETIRED 2026-07-28 (docs/multi-user-crew.html P1.3.3). Nothing
# reads it; setting it has no effect. Its one production consumer was
# lib/owner.ts's env-pin branch, deleted along with the lowest-cuid sole-row
# fallback. Ownership is now a STORED FACT — `User.role = 'owner'` — and
# resolveOwner() is a single findFirst({ where: { role: 'owner' } }). Do not
# reintroduce it: an env pin and a role column disagreeing is a silent
# authorization split. Provision people by adding User rows (role defaults to
# "crew"), not by editing env.
